# The ledger of `s3`: what a program does with the S3 API, measured
# against the census the author set on 2026-11-17: Python's boto3 1.44.6
# and botocore 1.44.6 (installed with `pip --target` into the scratch
# folder on 2026-11-19, walked with `dir()` of an S3 client and of
# `botocore.auth`, its 116 operations read from the client's service
# model), Go's github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/s3 v1.116.0 with
# `aws/signer/v4` of aws-sdk-go-v2 v1.48.0, `config` v1.33.20 and
# `feature/s3/manager` v1.23.27, and github.com/minio/minio-go/v7 v7.4.1
# (`go doc -all`, fetched into the scratch folder the same day), and
# Rust's aws-sdk-s3 1.161.0 and rust-s3 0.38.0 (their docs.rs pages of
# `Client` and `Bucket`, read the same day; no crate was built).
#
# The expected values were observed: botocore 1.44.6 signed the vectors of
# AWS's SigV4 test suite (service `service`, us-east-1,
# 2015-08-30T12:36:00Z) and the S3 reference's examples (2013-05-24), with
# its clock held, and answered every canonical request, string to sign,
# signature and presigned URL the tests hold; botocore, Go's SDK and
# minio-go were asked which URL each reaches a bucket and a key by; Go's
# hash/crc32 and hash/crc64 answered the checksums, CRC-64/NVME by the
# polynomial 0x9a6c9329ac4bc9b5; botocore read the shared files of the
# tests. The live runs reached MinIO built from the commit of its release
# RELEASE.2025-10-15T17-29-55Z (Go module v0.0.0-20251015172955-9e49d5e7a648,
# its server naming its version DEVELOPMENT.GOGET), the image
# alpine/minio:RELEASE.2025-10-15T17-29-55Z (224 MB), since Docker Hub
# answers no repository minio/minio and quay.io no manifest of seven of its
# release tags (asked 2026-11-19); in a container of this Mac, by path, at
# http://127.0.0.1:9000, named to the tests by REBEL_S3_LIVE_URL.

capability auth/sign  a request to S3 or any AWS service signed by Signature Version 4, in its header
  heroes  s3.sign_request, s3.Signature
  go      v4.NewSigner, v4.Signer.SignHTTP, v4.SignHTTPRequestMiddleware, v4.NewSignHTTPRequestMiddleware, v4.SigningError, v4.GetSignedRequestSignature
  rust    -
  python  botocore.auth.SigV4Auth, botocore.auth.S3SigV4Auth
  note    S3's rules hold for the service `s3`, as botocore's two classes divide them: the path encoded once and never normalized, `x-amz-content-sha256` sent; every other service's path is normalized and encoded again, as the test suite asks. Every field is signed but those a connection or a proxy may write, as botocore signs; Go's SDK signs a fixed list

capability auth/parts  the canonical request, the key derived from the secret, the date and the encoding of SigV4, each alone
  heroes  s3.canonical_request, s3.signing_key, s3.format_amz_date, s3.parse_amz_date, s3.uri_encode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.canonical_request, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.string_to_sign, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.signature, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.canonical_headers, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.signed_headers, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.payload, botocore.auth.SigV4Auth.credential_scope
  note    A query's `+` is read as a space before it is encoded again, as Go's SDK and Python's parse_qsl read it; botocore signs a URL's query as written

capability auth/presign  a request presigned in its URL's query, for any service
  heroes  s3.presign_request
  go      v4.Signer.PresignHTTP, v4.PresignHTTPRequestMiddleware, v4.NewPresignHTTPRequestMiddleware
  rust    -
  python  botocore.auth.SigV4QueryAuth, botocore.auth.S3SigV4QueryAuth
  note    S3's payload is `UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD`, any other service's the SHA-256 of the body, as botocore presigns; a token is in the query

capability auth/payload  a body sent unsigned over HTTPS, its integrity left to TLS and the checksums
  heroes  s3.Client, s3.UNSIGNED_PAYLOAD, s3.EMPTY_PAYLOAD_SHA256
  go      v4.AddUnsignedPayloadMiddleware, v4.SwapComputePayloadSHA256ForUnsignedPayloadMiddleware, v4.UseDynamicPayloadSigningMiddleware, v4.AddComputePayloadSHA256Middleware, v4.RemoveComputePayloadSHA256Middleware, v4.AddContentSHA256HeaderMiddleware, v4.RemoveContentSHA256HeaderMiddleware, v4.ComputePayloadSHA256, v4.ContentSHA256Header, v4.UnsignedPayload, v4.GetPayloadHash, v4.SetPayloadHash, v4.HashComputationError
  rust    -
  python  botocore.config.Config(s3={'payload_signing_enabled'})
  note    Signed by default, which every server checks; unsigned is refused over http://, where nothing else would keep the body whole

capability auth/streaming  a body signed chunk by chunk as it streams (aws-chunked)
  heroes  refused: a body of a length not known in advance goes in parts (`s3.put_object_from`), which every server of the API reads, holding one part in memory; the chunked signature adds a second way to the same upload
  go      v4.NewStreamSigner, v4.StreamSigner.GetSignature, v4.AddStreamingEventsPayload, v4.StreamingEventsPayload
  rust    -
  python  -

capability auth/sigv4a  a request signed by SigV4a for a multi-region access point
  heroes  pending: SigV4a signs with an ECDSA P-256 key derived from the secret, which needs a key pair made from a scalar the program computes; not written
  go      s3.WithSigV4ASigningRegions, s3.WithSigV4SigningName, s3.WithSigV4SigningRegion
  rust    -
  python  botocore.crt.auth (needs awscrt)

capability credentials/given  the keys given by the program, a session token among them
  heroes  s3.Credentials
  go      aws.Credentials, credentials.NewStaticCredentialsProvider
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::credentials_provider
  python  boto3.client(aws_access_key_id=, aws_secret_access_key=, aws_session_token=)
  note    minio-go credentials.NewStaticV4, rust-s3 Credentials::new and Bucket::credentials, set_credentials, access_key, secret_key, security_token, session_token

capability credentials/env  the keys read from the environment
  heroes  s3.credentials_from_env
  go      config.EnvConfig, config.NewEnvConfig
  rust    -
  python  botocore.credentials.EnvProvider
  note    One key set without the other is `invalid`, as botocore's PartialCredentialsError; minio-go credentials.NewEnvAWS and NewEnvMinio, rust-s3's aws-creds 0.40.0 Credentials::from_env and from_env_specific

capability credentials/files  the keys of a profile in the shared credentials and config files
  heroes  s3.credentials_from_text, s3.credentials_from_files, s3.SharedFile, s3.profile_name
  go      config.LoadSharedConfigProfile, config.SharedConfig, config.WithSharedConfigProfile, config.WithSharedCredentialsFiles, config.WithSharedConfigFiles, config.DefaultSharedCredentialsFilename, config.DefaultSharedConfigFilename, config.DefaultSharedConfigProfile
  rust    -
  python  botocore.credentials.SharedCredentialProvider, botocore.credentials.ConfigProvider, boto3.Session(profile_name=)
  note    The credentials file first, then the config file, as botocore; `[profile x]` in a credentials file and `[x]` in a config file name no profile x, as botocore 1.44.6 answered; a section given twice is read as one, as Go's SDK reads it, where botocore refuses the file. minio-go credentials.NewFileAWSCredentials, rust-s3's aws-creds Credentials::from_profile and from_credentials_file

capability credentials/chain  the keys a program finds by itself
  heroes  s3.default_credentials
  go      config.LoadDefaultConfig
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::credentials_provider
  python  boto3.Session.get_credentials, botocore.credentials.create_credential_resolver
  note    The environment, then the shared files; minio-go credentials.NewChainCredentials, rust-s3's aws-creds Credentials::default and anonymous

capability credentials/shown  credentials written for a person without the secret
  heroes  s3.format_credentials
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    The id's last four characters alone shown

capability credentials/vended  keys vended by another service: the instance's metadata, STS's roles assumed, web identity, single sign-on, a process
  heroes  refused: each is a client of a service other than S3 (IMDS, STS, SSO OIDC) or a program's; a program asks it with `s3.sign_request` or its own package and gives the keys it gets as `s3.Credentials`
  go      config.WithEC2IMDSRegion, config.WithAssumeRoleCredentialOptions, config.WithWebIdentityRoleCredentialOptions, config.WithSSOTokenProviderOptions, config.WithProcessCredentialOptions, config.WithCredentialsProvider
  rust    -
  python  botocore.credentials.InstanceMetadataProvider, botocore.credentials.AssumeRoleProvider, botocore.credentials.AssumeRoleWithWebIdentityProvider, botocore.credentials.SSOProvider, botocore.credentials.ProcessProvider, botocore.credentials.ContainerProvider, botocore.credentials.RefreshableCredentials
  note    minio-go credentials.NewIAM, NewSTSAssumeRole, NewSTSWebIdentity, NewLDAPIdentity, NewSTSClientGrants and the MinIO STS of its own; rust-s3's aws-creds Credentials::from_sts, from_sts_env, from_instance_metadata, from_instance_metadata_v2, from_container_credentials_provider, refresh, and its Bucket::credentials_refresh

capability config/profile  what a profile of the config file says of S3: region, endpoint, addressing
  heroes  s3.profile_from_text, s3.profile_from_files, s3.Profile
  go      config.SharedConfig, config.LoadSharedConfigProfile
  rust    -
  python  botocore.session.Session.get_scoped_config, botocore.session.Session.get_config_variable
  note    `services` sections and their nested `s3 = endpoint_url` before the profile's own `endpoint_url`, as botocore 1.44.6 answered

capability client/env  a client made from the environment and the shared files
  heroes  s3.client_from_env
  go      s3.NewFromConfig, config.LoadDefaultConfig
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::new
  python  boto3.client('s3')
  note    AWS_REGION, AWS_DEFAULT_REGION, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3 and AWS_ENDPOINT_URL before the profile, as botocore reads them; us-east-1 where none names a region

capability client/aws  a client of Amazon S3 in a region, by its regional endpoint
  heroes  s3.aws_client, s3.Client
  go      s3.New, s3.Options, s3.NewDefaultEndpointResolverV2, s3.NewDefaultEndpointResolver
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::from_conf, aws_sdk_s3::Client::config
  python  boto3.client('s3', region_name=)
  note    us-east-1 is reached at s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com, as Go's SDK and minio-go reach it, where botocore's legacy default uses s3.amazonaws.com; a `cn-` region ends `.amazonaws.com.cn`, as all three answered; rust-s3 Bucket::new, Region

capability client/endpoint  a client of any server of the S3 API: MinIO, R2, Backblaze B2, a base path kept
  heroes  s3.minio_client, s3.r2_client, s3.backblaze_client, s3.Client
  go      s3.Options.BaseEndpoint, s3.EndpointResolverFromURL, s3.WithEndpointResolver, s3.WithEndpointResolverV2
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::endpoint_url
  python  boto3.client('s3', endpoint_url=)
  note    minio-go New and EndpointURL, rust-s3 Region::Custom, Region::R2, Bucket::new_public, Bucket::region, Bucket::scheme

capability client/addressing  a bucket reached by its host name or by path
  heroes  s3.Addressing, s3.object_url, s3.is_bucket_name
  go      s3.Options.UsePathStyle
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::force_path_style
  python  botocore.config.Config(s3={'addressing_style'})
  note    Amazon's endpoints by host name, any other by path, as botocore and minio-go choose where Go's SDK reaches every endpoint by host name; a name with a dot by path under HTTPS, as all three; rust-s3 with_path_style, set_path_style, set_subdomain_style, is_path_style, is_subdomain_style, path_style_host, subdomain_style_host, host, url, name, create_with_path_style; minio-go Options.BucketLookup

capability client/dualstack-accelerate  Amazon's dual-stack and Transfer Acceleration endpoints
  heroes  s3.Client
  go      s3.Options.EndpointOptions, s3.Options.UseAccelerate
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::use_dual_stack, aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::accelerate
  python  botocore.config.Config(s3={'use_dualstack_endpoint', 'use_accelerate_endpoint'})
  note    minio-go SetS3EnableDualstack, SetS3TransferAccelerate

capability client/fips  Amazon's FIPS endpoints
  heroes  pending: the hosts `s3-fips.<region>.amazonaws.com` are known; the field that chooses them is not written
  go      s3.Options.EndpointOptions
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::use_fips
  python  botocore.config.Config(use_fips_endpoint=)

capability client/limits  a time limit, retries, the authorities trusted and the size of an answer
  heroes  s3.Client
  go      s3.Options.Retryer, s3.Options.RetryMaxAttempts, s3.Options.RetryMode, s3.Options.HTTPClient
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::retry_config, aws_sdk_s3::config::Builder::timeout_config
  python  botocore.config.Config(retries=, connect_timeout=, read_timeout=)
  note    Three attempts in all, as botocore 1.44.6's standard mode and Go's retry.DefaultMaxAttempts answered, after 500, 502, 503, 504, 429, SlowDown and RequestTimeout and a connection that failed, the waits 100 ms doubled; rust-s3 set_request_timeout, request_timeout, with_request_timeout; minio-go Options.Transport

capability client/proxy  requests sent through a proxy
  heroes  pending: `http` reaches a proxy only from its own client, which undoes a stored `Content-Encoding`; S3's bytes must come as stored, so requests go on a connection of this package's own, which knows no proxy
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  botocore.config.Config(proxies=)
  note    rust-s3 set_proxy; Go's through its HTTPClient

capability client/raw  any operation of the API signed and sent, or signed and handed to the program's transport
  heroes  s3.operation, s3.Operation, s3.prepare, s3.send
  go      s3.WithAPIOptions, s3.Options.APIOptions
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::config
  python  boto3.client.meta, botocore.awsrequest.AWSRequest
  note    rust-s3 add_header, add_query, extra_headers, extra_headers_mut, extra_query, extra_query_mut, with_extra_headers, with_extra_query, http_client

capability objects/put  an object put with its type, fields, metadata, tags, storage class, ACL, encryption and conditions
  heroes  s3.put_object, s3.put_options, s3.PutOptions, s3.headers_of, s3.Stored, s3.Meta, s3.Tag
  go      s3.Client.PutObject, s3.PutObjectInput
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::put_object
  python  S3.Client.put_object
  note    `if_none_match` `*` refused is `exists`; a CRC-32 is sent by default, as botocore 1.44.6 sends `x-amz-checksum-crc32` (its `request_checksum_calculation` `when_supported`, measured against MinIO); minio-go PutObject, PutObjectOptions; rust-s3 put_object, put_object_with_content_type, put_object_with_headers, put_object_with_content_type_and_headers, put_object_builder

capability objects/get  an object read whole, by a range, by version, by part and on conditions
  heroes  s3.get_object, s3.get_object_with, s3.get_options, s3.GetOptions, s3.Range, s3.range_header, s3.get_operation, s3.Object, s3.Info
  go      s3.Client.GetObject, s3.GetObjectInput
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::get_object
  python  S3.Client.get_object
  note    An object stored with a `Content-Encoding` is answered as stored, as all three; not modified (304) and a condition not met (412) are `failed`; minio-go GetObject, GetObjectOptions.SetRange; rust-s3 get_object, get_object_range

capability objects/get-stream  an object of any size written to a stream as it comes
  heroes  s3.get_object_to
  go      manager.NewDownloader, manager.Downloader.Download
  rust    -
  python  S3.Client.download_file, S3.Client.download_fileobj
  note    One connection, read in order; Go's downloader reads ranges on threads at once (`objects/get-parallel`); minio-go FGetObject; rust-s3 get_object_to_writer, get_object_range_to_writer, get_object_stream

capability objects/get-parallel  an object read by ranges on several connections at once
  heroes  pending: a range read on each thread needs a thread to carry a client and a range, which the language's threads (an i64 or a str each way) do only through text; not written
  go      manager.Downloader.Concurrency, manager.Downloader.PartSize, manager.WithDownloaderClientOptions, manager.WriteAtBuffer, manager.NewWriteAtBuffer
  rust    -
  python  boto3.s3.transfer.TransferConfig, s3transfer.manager.TransferManager

capability objects/head  what S3 says of an object without its bytes
  heroes  s3.head_object, s3.info_of
  go      s3.Client.HeadObject
  rust    aws_sdk_s3::Client::head_object
  python  S3.Client.head_object
  note    minio-go StatObject, ToObjectInfo; rust-s3 head_object

capability objects/exists  whether an object is there
  heroes  s3.object_exists
  go      s3.NewObjectExistsWaiter, s3.NewObjectNotExistsWaiter
  rust    -
  python  S3.Client.get_waiter('object_exists')
  note    rust-s3 object_exists; a waiter is `object_exists` called again by the program, which sleeps by `time`

capability objects/delete  an object deleted, or one version of it
